.TH TRESTLECTL 8 "October 2026" "trestle 0.2" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
trestlectl \- inspect and clean up trestle attachments
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B trestlectl status
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B trestlectl list
.RB [ \-n
.IR network ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B trestlectl leases
.RB [ \-n
.IR network ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B trestlectl gc
.RB [ \-n
.IR network ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B trestlectl detach
.I network container-id
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B trestlectl
asks
.BR trestled (8)
about the networks and attachments it manages. It is the trestle client
binary (a symlink to it), so it talks to the daemon over the same socket
and passes the same identity checks.
.TP
.B status
Networks (configured, created from \fB[network:*]\fR, or with state left),
their bridge (\fB*\fR = currently absent), subnet, IPAM mode, isolation,
anti-spoofing, number of attachments, free addresses and name publication
(\fBDNS\fR: \fBaardvark\fR, \fBdnsmasq\fR, \fBhosts\fR, or \fBoff\fR).
Below the table: whether aardvark-dns is running and whether it runs in its
systemd scope \fBtrestle-aardvark-dns.scope\fR (from \fI/proc/<pid>/cgroup\fR;
\fBOUTSIDE\fR means it was started some other way), or \fBNOT RUNNING\fR
while networks need it; and for every network with \fBdnsmasq_hosts\fR the
hosts file and whether the dnsmasq from \fBdnsmasq_pid_file\fR runs.
Non-root users see the networks they may use.
.TP
.B list
Attachments: network, container id and name, user, host and container
interface, IP, MAC and state (\fBup\fR, \fBSTALE\fR when the host veth no
longer exists, \fBdhcp\fR / \fBdhcp-no-keeper\fR). Non-root users see only
their own attachments.
.TP
.B leases
IP leases and whether an attachment still holds them (\fBORPHANED\fR
otherwise). Root only.
.TP
.B gc
Run the stale state sweep now (see \fBgc_interval\fR in
.BR trestle.conf (5)):
remove attachments whose host veth is gone, release orphaned leases and
restart lost DHCP keepers. \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR only reports. Root only.
.TP
.B detach
Remove an attachment as a teardown would (hook script, DHCP release, veth,
lease, DNS entries). For containers the runtime forgot about. Users may
detach their own containers, root any.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-network " \fInetwork\fR"
Limit to one network.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the daemon's JSON answer instead of a table.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 when the daemon refused or failed the request, 2 on usage
errors.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# trestlectl status
# trestlectl list -n dev
# trestlectl gc --dry-run
# trestlectl detach dev 3f2a9c...
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR trestle (8),
.BR trestled (8),
.BR trestle.conf (5)
